# Testing the website

The website's tests draw its pages and read the markup that comes out. They render through Foldkit's server renderer, the same path the server takes when it answers a request. So a case sees the page a visitor would get, down to its classes and its inline styles.

The cases fall into three files. The page tests cover every route and the pieces the landing is made of. The documentation tests cover the shell that draws the woven docs. The example tests cover the Loom document the landing types out. A small module of helpers serves all three.

## Drawing a piece of the page

Foldkit hands an `HtmlBuilder` only to a view, and a test has no view of its own. So `renderDocument` wraps whatever the case wants to draw in a one-off application, whose model is `null` and whose view is the drawing itself. It renders that application to a string, without the markers a browser would need to hydrate it.

Most cases draw a fragment rather than a document. `renderHtml` gives the fragment an empty title and returns its markup alone.

=>

export const renderDocument = (
  draw: (h: HtmlBuilder<Message>) => Document,
): Server.RenderedApplication =>
  Effect.runSync(
    Server.renderToString(
      { init: () => ({ model: null }), view: (_: null, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>) => draw(h) },
      { isHydratable: false },
    ),
  )

export const renderHtml = (draw: (h: HtmlBuilder<Message>) => Html): string =>
  renderDocument((h) => ({ title: '', body: draw(h) })).html

## A model to draw from

Every view reads the site's `Model`, so every case needs one. `modelAt` gives the model a visitor has before they touch anything: on the landing, with Bun as the runtime, the palette shut, and the example at rest. A case passes only the fields it cares about, and they override the rest.

=>

export const modelAt = (over: Partial<Model> = {}): Model => ({
  route: 'home',
  chapter: '',
  drawerOpen: false,
  runtime: 'bun',
  searchOpen: false,
  caret: 0,
  searching: false,
  found: [],
  activeSection: '',
  player: { struck: 0, beat: 0, playing: false, output: Option.none(), full: false },
  version: '0.0.9',
  query: '',
  exact: false,
  focus: 0,
  copied: '',
  book: Option.none(),
  ...over,
})

## Anchors written in two pieces

Some cases look for an anchor in the markup, because the site draws a loom's anchors as links. An anchor written out in this chapter's code would be read as an anchor of this chapter, and the tangle would fail on a section that does not exist. So `anchor` spells it in two pieces, which the page receives whole.

=>

export const anchor = (name: string): string => `::${'['}${name}]`

## The helpers module {Tangle}[render.ts]

Effect runs the renderer and supplies `Option`. Foldkit supplies the server renderer and the builder's types. The site's own model supplies the shape every view reads.

=>

import { Effect, Option } from 'effect'
import { Server } from 'foldkit/experimental'
import type { Document, Html, HtmlBuilder } from 'foldkit/html'
import type { Message, Model } from '../src/model'

::[Drawing a piece of the page]

